---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";

const props = {
	title: "SahraAI",
	tagline:
		"Joinable-table discovery and intent-driven enrichment over heterogeneous data lakes.",
	description:
		"A data discovery system that goes past finding tables: it actually populates new attributes into the base dataset at row level, with provenance.",
	status: "in-progress" as const,
	startDate: "2025",
	type: "research" as const,
	role: "Technical implementer",
	team: "Team project (members credited later); reviewed with Dr. Omar Alomair",
	stack: ["Python", "PostgreSQL", "Embeddings", "LLMs"],
	links: {
		repo: "https://github.com/gosamab/sahra",
	},
	relatedProjects: [
		{ slug: "makhtotah-research", title: "Makhtotah Research" },
		{ slug: "arabic-ocr-hwr", title: "OCR/HWR, Qwen2" },
	],
	toc: [
		{ id: "the-problem", label: "The problem" },
		{ id: "what-makes-it-different", label: "What makes it different" },
		{ id: "method", label: "Method" },
		{ id: "where-it-fits", label: "Where it fits" },
	],
};
---

<ProjectLayout {...props}>
	<h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
	<p>
		Data lakes are full of structured, semi-structured, and unstructured
		datasets sitting in raw form. The hard part isn't storing them; it's
		<em>finding</em> the ones that meaningfully extend a dataset you already
		have. Existing work is split across <strong>joinable</strong>
		discovery (JOSIE-style overlap search) and <strong>unionable</strong>
		discovery (DUST-style novelty maximization): both stop at retrieval.
	</p>

	<h2 id="what-makes-it-different">What makes it different</h2>
	<p>
		SahraAI targets the gap between <em>discovery</em> and <em>enrichment</em>.
		The deliverable isn't "here are 10 candidate tables", it's "here is
		your base dataset with new attributes populated at the row level, with
		provenance and confidence." Discovery, schema normalization, candidate
		join inference, and value population live in one pipeline.
	</p>

	<h2 id="method">Method</h2>
	<p>
		Each candidate relation in the lake is scored by a utility function
		<code>S(Q, R) = 0.4C + 0.3R + 0.2P + 0.1N</code>:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Coverage (C)</strong>: how much of the query table the join actually matches.</li>
		<li><strong>Semantic Relevance (R)</strong>: embedding/ontology/LLM similarity over join attributes.</li>
		<li><strong>Plausibility (P)</strong>: joined tuples that obey unit and domain constraints.</li>
		<li><strong>Novelty (N)</strong>: values <em>R</em> introduces beyond what <em>Q</em> already contains.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The pipeline minimizes physical access to lake tables by leaning on
		metadata-driven similarity (schemas, statistics, sketches) and only
		reaching into a bounded candidate subset when it has to.
	</p>

	<h2 id="where-it-fits">Where it fits</h2>
	<p>
		Builds on the conceptual frame from Nargesian et al. (data lake
		management as on-demand query answering) and Fan et al.'s table
		discovery taxonomy; complements JOSIE (joinable retrieval at scale)
		and DUST (novelty-driven unionable retrieval). The contribution is in
		not stopping at retrieval, turning discovery into a usable enriched
		dataset ready for downstream modeling.
	</p>
</ProjectLayout>
